# Applications: Gemini Rate Limit Implementation Plan

## Objective

Prevent Gemini API overuse and make chatbot behavior predictable when quotas are hit by adding:
- pre-request quota checks
- per-model request tracking
- model fallback
- 429-aware retry with backoff
- user-facing rate-limit feedback

## Scope

Files in `Applications/src`:
- `config/chatbot-config.js`
- `utils/rate-limiter.js`
- `services/gemini-client.js`
- `services/chatbot-service.js`
- `components/chatbot/ChatWindow.jsx` (optional UX hardening)

Out of scope:
- backend-side centralized quota enforcement
- per-user server quotas

## Implementation Phases

### Phase 1: Configuration Baseline

Update `chatbot-config.js` with explicit rate-limit and retry settings:
- define model chain (`id`, `rpm`, `rpd`, `label`)
- define retry settings (`maxAttempts`, `baseDelayMs`, `maxDelayMs`, `cooldownMs`)
- keep values aligned with currently used Gemini tier

Acceptance criteria:
- config contains all model limits and retry knobs in one place
- no hardcoded limit values remain in service code

### Phase 2: Client Rate Limiter

Implement/verify `utils/rate-limiter.js`:
- sliding-window tracking for RPM (60s) and RPD (24h)
- per-model storage shape: `timestamps[]`, `cooldownUntil`
- `canRequestModel(modelId, rpm, rpd)`
- `recordRequest(modelId)`
- `markCooldown(modelId, durationMs)`
- `findAvailableModel(modelChain)`
- `getRemainingQuota(modelChain)` and `getQuotaSummary()`
- persist state to `localStorage`

Acceptance criteria:
- limiter blocks requests that exceed rpm/rpd
- cooldown is respected after 429
- state survives page refresh

### Phase 3: Gemini Client Resilience

Implement/verify `services/gemini-client.js`:
- select first available model from chain
- on 429:
  - parse `Retry-After` header when present
  - mark model cooldown
  - immediately try next model
- if last model still 429: retry with exponential backoff + jitter
- return structured error fields:
  - `isRateLimited`
  - `retryAfterMs`
  - `modelUsed` on success

Acceptance criteria:
- fallback happens without user-visible crash
- last-model retries stop at configured max attempts
- response payload always carries enough metadata for UI handling

### Phase 4: Chatbot Service Integration

Implement/verify `services/chatbot-service.js`:
- pre-check model availability before calling Gemini
- map rate-limit failures into user-safe error messages
- expose `getRateLimitInfo()` for UI diagnostics

Acceptance criteria:
- service returns consistent `{ success, error, isRateLimited, retryAfterMs }`
- no raw API error leaks directly to users

### Phase 5: UX Handling (Recommended)

Improve `components/chatbot/ChatWindow.jsx`:
- detect `isRateLimited` state from service response
- show cooldown-aware message with retry hint (seconds remaining)
- disable send button briefly when `retryAfterMs > 0` (optional)

Acceptance criteria:
- users see actionable feedback when limited
- repeated spam-clicking is reduced

## Verification Plan

## 1. Functional checks

- Send normal messages until near quota and confirm successful responses.
- Force RPM breach and confirm request is blocked before network call.
- Simulate 429 from one model and confirm automatic fallback to next model.
- Simulate 429 on final model and confirm backoff retry sequence.
- Confirm response contains `isRateLimited` and `retryAfterMs` when limited.

## 2. Persistence checks

- Trigger quota usage, refresh app, confirm limiter state is retained.
- Clear storage/reset limiter and confirm quotas reset correctly.

## 3. Build checks

Run:

```bash
cd Applications
npm run build
```

Expected:
- build exits with code 0
- no runtime import/export errors from chatbot files

## Rollout Notes

- Start with conservative limits for free tier.
- Monitor user-visible rate-limit frequency for 3-7 days.
- Tune `modelChain` ordering and retry timings based on observed behavior.

## Current Status (as of 2026-03-08)

Core implementation is already present in the codebase and this plan now serves as the execution/verification checklist for stabilization and rollout.
